// Bondline reaction guide: one entry per reaction (structures checked with RDKit).
window.BL_GUIDE={rx:[{"id":"g-radchlor","cat":"Radicals and alkanes","name":"Free-radical chlorination of an alkane","s":"C1CCCCC1","r":"Cl₂, UV light","p":"ClC1CCCCC1","sum":"UV light splits Cl₂ into chlorine radicals, which swap a hydrogen on the alkane for chlorine in a chain reaction.","k":["Three stages: initiation (Cl₂ → 2Cl•), propagation (two steps that repeat) and termination (two radicals combine).","Chlorine is unselective, so an alkane with different kinds of hydrogen gives a mixture of products.","Excess chlorine gives further substitution (di-, tri-, tetrachloro products)."],"aka":"halogenation photochemical substitution"},{"id":"g-radbrom","cat":"Radicals and alkanes","name":"Free-radical bromination of an alkane","s":"CC(C)C","r":"Br₂, UV light","p":"CC(C)(C)Br","sum":"Bromine radicals are choosy: they take the hydrogen that leaves the most stable radical, so the tertiary position is brominated.","k":["Selectivity: tertiary > secondary > primary, because the radical stability follows the same order.","Bromination is far more selective than chlorination, so it usually gives one main product.","Uses single-headed (fish-hook) arrows: each one moves a single electron."]},{"id":"g-allylbrom","cat":"Radicals and alkanes","name":"Allylic bromination with NBS","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"NBS, light or peroxide","p":"BrC1C=CCCC1","sum":"NBS puts bromine on the carbon next to a C=C double bond, leaving the double bond in place.","k":["Goes through an allylic radical, which is stabilised by resonance.","NBS keeps the Br₂ concentration very low, so Br₂ does not add across the double bond.","An unsymmetrical allylic radical can give two products, because the radical is shared over two carbons."],"aka":"N-bromosuccinimide Wohl-Ziegler"},{"id":"g-benzbrom","cat":"Radicals and alkanes","name":"Benzylic bromination with NBS","s":"Cc1ccccc1","r":"NBS, light or peroxide","p":"BrCc1ccccc1","sum":"The carbon attached directly to a benzene ring is brominated, because the benzylic radical is stabilised by the ring.","k":["Only the benzylic position reacts; the ring itself is untouched.","Needs at least one benzylic hydrogen.","The product is a useful SN2 substrate."],"aka":"N-bromosuccinimide side chain"},{"id":"g-radhbr","cat":"Radicals and alkanes","name":"Anti-Markovnikov addition of HBr (peroxide effect)","s":"CC=C","r":"HBr, peroxide (ROOR)","p":"CCCBr","sum":"With a peroxide present, HBr adds by a radical chain and bromine ends up on the less substituted carbon.","k":["Br• adds first, to the end of the C=C that gives the more stable carbon radical.","The result is the opposite of the ionic addition, which follows Markovnikov’s rule.","Works only for HBr, not HCl or HI."],"aka":"Kharasch radical addition"},{"id":"g-rx-oh","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Halogenoalkane to alcohol (hydrolysis)","s":"CCCBr","r":"NaOH(aq), heat","p":"CCCO","sum":"Hydroxide replaces the halogen. Primary halogenoalkanes react by SN2, tertiary ones by SN1.","k":["Use aqueous NaOH; in ethanol, elimination takes over.","Rate: C–I > C–Br > C–Cl > C–F, because the weaker C–X bond breaks more easily.","SN2 inverts the stereocentre; SN1 gives a mixture of both configurations."],"m":"sn2","aka":"nucleophilic substitution"},{"id":"g-rx-cn","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Halogenoalkane to nitrile","s":"CCBr","r":"KCN, ethanol, heat","p":"CCC#N","sum":"Cyanide replaces the halogen and adds one carbon to the chain.","k":["A key way to lengthen a carbon chain by one.","The nitrile can then be hydrolysed to a carboxylic acid or reduced to an amine.","SN2: works best for methyl and primary halogenoalkanes."],"m":"cnsub"},{"id":"g-rx-n3","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Halogenoalkane to azide","s":"CCCBr","r":"NaN₃, DMF","p":"CCCN=[N+]=[N-]","sum":"Azide ion is an excellent nucleophile and gives an alkyl azide by SN2.","k":["Reducing the azide (LiAlH₄, or H₂ with Pd) gives a primary amine with no over-alkylation.","Inverts the stereocentre."]},{"id":"g-rx-sh","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Halogenoalkane to thiol","s":"CCBr","r":"NaSH","p":"CCS","sum":"Hydrosulfide (HS⁻) replaces the halogen by SN2 to give a thiol.","k":["Sulfur nucleophiles are strong nucleophiles but weak bases, so substitution beats elimination.","Use excess NaSH so the thiol does not react again to give a sulfide."]},{"id":"g-finkel","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Finkelstein reaction","s":"CCCCCl","r":"NaI, acetone","p":"CCCCI","sum":"Iodide swaps for chloride or bromide by SN2.","k":["NaI dissolves in acetone but NaCl and NaBr do not, so the salt precipitates and pulls the reaction forward.","Alkyl fluorides are made the other way round with metal fluorides (Swarts reaction)."],"aka":"halogen exchange Swarts"},{"id":"g-solv","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Solvolysis of a tertiary halogenoalkane (SN1)","s":"CC(C)(C)Br","r":"H₂O","p":"CC(C)(C)O","sum":"The C–Br bond breaks first to give a carbocation, then water attacks and loses a proton.","k":["Rate depends only on the halogenoalkane: rate = k[RX].","Favoured by tertiary substrates and polar protic solvents.","Carbocations can rearrange, and some elimination (E1) always competes."],"m":"sn1"},{"id":"g-e2-zait","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"E2 elimination (Zaitsev product)","s":"CCC(C)Br","r":"NaOEt, EtOH, heat","p":"C/C=C/C","sum":"A strong base removes a hydrogen from the carbon next to C–Br while bromide leaves, in one step.","k":["Zaitsev’s rule: the more substituted alkene is the major product, and the trans (E) isomer is favoured.","The H and the leaving group must be anti-periplanar.","Rate = k[RX][base]."],"m":"e2","aka":"dehydrohalogenation Saytzeff"},{"id":"g-e2-hof","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"E2 with a bulky base (Hofmann product)","s":"CCC(C)(C)Br","r":"KOtBu, tBuOH","p":"CCC(C)=C","sum":"A bulky base cannot reach the crowded hydrogen, so it takes the most accessible one and gives the less substituted alkene.","k":["Potassium tert-butoxide and LDA are the usual bulky bases.","Compare with ethoxide, which gives the Zaitsev alkene."],"aka":"tert-butoxide"},{"id":"g-e1","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"E1 elimination","s":"CCC(C)(C)Br","r":"EtOH, heat","p":"CC=C(C)C","sum":"The leaving group goes first to give a carbocation, then a weak base removes a neighbouring proton.","k":["Tertiary substrates, weak base, heat.","Gives the Zaitsev alkene. SN1 substitution always competes.","No anti-periplanar requirement, because the carbocation is flat."],"m":"e1"},{"id":"g-grig-make","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Making a Grignard reagent","s":"CCBr","r":"Mg, dry ether","p":"CC[Mg]Br","sum":"Magnesium inserts into the C–X bond, turning an electrophilic carbon into a strongly nucleophilic one.","k":["Everything must be dry: Grignard reagents are destroyed by water, alcohols and acids.","Works for alkyl, vinyl and aryl halides.","Ether or THF solvent stabilises the magnesium."],"aka":"organomagnesium"},{"id":"g-rli-make","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Making an organolithium reagent","s":"CCCCBr","r":"2 Li, hexane","p":"[Li]CCCC","sum":"Two equivalents of lithium turn a halogenoalkane into an organolithium, a very strong base and nucleophile.","k":["Even more reactive than a Grignard reagent.","Butyllithium is used to make LDA and to deprotonate terminal alkynes."]},{"id":"g-gilman","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Coupling with a Gilman reagent (Corey–House)","s":"CCCCCCBr","r":"(CH₃)₂CuLi","p":"CCCCCCC","sum":"A lithium dialkylcuprate replaces the halogen with one of its alkyl groups, making a new C–C bond.","k":["Works with primary alkyl, vinyl and aryl halides.","Gilman reagents are softer than Grignard reagents: they do not add to ketones, but they do add 1,4 to enones."],"aka":"organocuprate Corey-House"},{"id":"g-wurtz","cat":"Halogenoalkanes","name":"Wurtz reaction","s":"CCBr.CCBr","r":"Na, dry ether","p":"CCCC","sum":"Sodium couples two halogenoalkanes into a longer alkane.","k":["Only useful for making symmetrical alkanes; two different halides give a mixture of three products.","Aryl halides couple in the same way (Fittig), and aryl with alkyl is the Wurtz–Fittig reaction."],"aka":"Fittig"},{"id":"g-ene-hx","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Addition of HBr to an alkene","s":"CC=C","r":"HBr","p":"CC(C)Br","sum":"The C=C attacks H⁺ to give the more stable carbocation, which bromide then captures.","k":["Markovnikov’s rule: H goes to the carbon that already has more hydrogens.","HCl and HI add the same way.","Not stereoselective: bromide can attack either face of the flat carbocation."],"m":"hbr","aka":"hydrohalogenation electrophilic addition"},{"id":"g-ene-rearr","cat":"Alkenes","name":"HX addition with a hydride shift","s":"CC(C)C=C","r":"HCl","p":"CCC(C)(C)Cl","sum":"The secondary carbocation formed first rearranges to a tertiary one before chloride attacks.","k":["A hydrogen (or a methyl group) moves with its bonding pair to the neighbouring C⁺.","Expect a rearrangement whenever a more stable carbocation is one shift away."],"aka":"carbocation rearrangement Wagner-Meerwein 1,2-shift"},{"id":"g-ene-h2o","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Acid-catalysed hydration of an alkene","s":"CC=C","r":"H₃O⁺ (dilute H₂SO₄)","p":"CC(C)O","sum":"Water adds across the double bond, with OH on the more substituted carbon.","k":["Markovnikov addition through a carbocation, so rearrangements are possible.","Industrially: steam and a phosphoric acid catalyst at 300 °C and 60 atm.","Reversible: it is the reverse of alcohol dehydration."],"m":"hydration"},{"id":"g-oxymerc","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Oxymercuration–demercuration","s":"CCCCC=C","r":"1. Hg(OAc)₂, H₂O  2. NaBH₄","p":"CCCCC(C)O","sum":"Markovnikov hydration without a free carbocation, so there is no rearrangement.","k":["A three-membered mercurinium ion forms, and water opens it at the more substituted carbon.","NaBH₄ then replaces mercury with hydrogen.","With an alcohol instead of water the product is an ether (alkoxymercuration)."]},{"id":"g-alkoxymerc","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Alkoxymercuration–demercuration","s":"CCCC=C","r":"1. Hg(OAc)₂, CH₃OH  2. NaBH₄","p":"CCCC(C)OC","sum":"The same reaction as oxymercuration but with an alcohol as the nucleophile, giving an ether.","k":["Markovnikov: the OR group ends up on the more substituted carbon.","No rearrangement."]},{"id":"g-hydrobor","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Hydroboration–oxidation","s":"CC1=CCCCC1","r":"1. BH₃·THF  2. H₂O₂, NaOH","p":"C[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","sum":"Anti-Markovnikov hydration: OH ends up on the less substituted carbon, and H and OH add to the same face.","k":["Boron adds to the less hindered carbon in one concerted step, so there is no carbocation and no rearrangement.","Syn addition: here the H and OH are cis, which puts the methyl and OH trans.","Oxidation replaces boron with OH, keeping the same stereochemistry."],"aka":"borane 9-BBN"},{"id":"g-ene-br2","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Addition of bromine to an alkene","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"Br₂, CH₂Cl₂","p":"Br[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","sum":"Bromine adds across the double bond through a cyclic bromonium ion, giving the trans dibromide.","k":["Anti addition: bromide opens the bromonium ion from the opposite face.","The orange-brown colour of bromine disappears: the test for a C=C bond.","Cl₂ reacts the same way."],"m":"br2","aka":"halogenation bromine water test unsaturation"},{"id":"g-halohydrin","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Halohydrin formation","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"Br₂, H₂O","p":"O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","sum":"In water, the bromonium ion is opened by water instead of bromide, giving a bromo alcohol.","k":["Anti addition: Br and OH end up trans.","Water attacks the more substituted carbon of the bromonium ion, so OH goes where Markovnikov would put it.","Base turns a halohydrin into an epoxide."],"aka":"bromohydrin"},{"id":"g-epox","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Epoxidation with a peroxyacid","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"mCPBA","p":"C1CCC2OC2C1","sum":"A peroxyacid delivers one oxygen atom to the double bond in a single concerted step.","k":["Syn addition: both C–O bonds form on the same face, so a cis alkene gives a cis epoxide.","More substituted (more electron-rich) alkenes react faster."],"m":"epoxidation","aka":"peracid oxirane"},{"id":"g-antidiol","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Anti dihydroxylation","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"1. mCPBA  2. H₃O⁺","p":"O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","sum":"Epoxidation followed by acid-catalysed ring opening with water gives a trans 1,2-diol.","k":["Water attacks the protonated epoxide from the opposite face.","Gives the opposite stereochemistry to OsO₄ or cold KMnO₄."],"aka":"trans diol glycol"},{"id":"g-syndiol","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Syn dihydroxylation","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"OsO₄ (cat.), NMO; or cold, dilute KMnO₄","p":"O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","sum":"Both OH groups are delivered to the same face of the double bond, giving a cis 1,2-diol.","k":["Goes through a cyclic osmate (or manganate) ester.","Cold dilute alkaline KMnO₄ turns from purple to brown: Baeyer’s test for unsaturation.","Hot, acidified KMnO₄ goes further and cleaves the C=C bond."],"aka":"osmium tetroxide Baeyer test glycol"},{"id":"g-ozon-red","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Ozonolysis (reductive work-up)","s":"CC(C)=CC","r":"1. O₃  2. Zn/CH₃COOH or (CH₃)₂S","p":"CC(C)=O.CC=O","sum":"Ozone cuts the C=C bond and puts a C=O on each carbon.","k":["A carbon with one H becomes an aldehyde; a carbon with no H becomes a ketone.","Useful for finding where a double bond was: join the two C=O carbons back together.","The reductive work-up keeps aldehydes as aldehydes."],"aka":"ozone cleavage DMS"},{"id":"g-ozon-ox","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Ozonolysis (oxidative work-up)","s":"CC(C)=CC","r":"1. O₃  2. H₂O₂","p":"CC(C)=O.CC(=O)O","sum":"With hydrogen peroxide in the work-up, any aldehyde formed is oxidised on to a carboxylic acid.","k":["Ketones are unchanged.","A terminal =CH₂ ends up as CO₂."]},{"id":"g-kmno4-cleave","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Oxidative cleavage with hot KMnO₄","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"KMnO₄, H₃O⁺, heat","p":"OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","sum":"Hot, concentrated, acidified manganate(VII) breaks the C=C bond completely.","k":["=CHR becomes a carboxylic acid, =CR₂ becomes a ketone, and =CH₂ becomes CO₂ and water.","A ring alkene opens into one chain with a C=O group at each end."],"aka":"potassium permanganate manganate(VII)"},{"id":"g-ene-h2","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Catalytic hydrogenation of an alkene","s":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","r":"H₂, Pd/C","p":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1C","sum":"Both hydrogens add from the metal surface to the same face of the double bond.","k":["Syn addition: here it gives the cis product.","Catalysts: Pd/C, Pt or Ni. Nickel at 150 °C is used to harden vegetable oils.","Less hindered alkenes react faster."],"aka":"reduction nickel platinum"},{"id":"g-simmons","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Simmons–Smith cyclopropanation","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"CH₂I₂, Zn(Cu)","p":"C1CCC2CC2C1","sum":"A zinc carbenoid adds a CH₂ group across the double bond to make a cyclopropane.","k":["Concerted and stereospecific: cis alkenes give cis cyclopropanes.","No free carbene is involved."],"aka":"carbenoid"},{"id":"g-carbene","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Dichlorocarbene addition","s":"C1=CCCCC1","r":"CHCl₃, KOtBu","p":"ClC1(Cl)C2CCCCC12","sum":"Base removes H⁺ then Cl⁻ from chloroform to give :CCl₂, which adds across the double bond.","k":["Gives a dichlorocyclopropane.","Stereospecific syn addition."],"aka":"carbene cyclopropanation"},{"id":"g-wacker","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Wacker oxidation","s":"CCCC=C","r":"PdCl₂ (cat.), CuCl₂, O₂, H₂O","p":"CCCC(C)=O","sum":"A terminal alkene is oxidised to a methyl ketone.","k":["Markovnikov: the oxygen ends up on the more substituted carbon.","Copper and oxygen re-oxidise the palladium so only a little is needed."]},{"id":"g-metathesis","cat":"Alkenes","name":"Ring-closing alkene metathesis","s":"C=CCCCCC=C","r":"Grubbs catalyst","p":"C1=CCCCC1.C=C","sum":"A ruthenium carbene swaps the ends of two C=C bonds. Here the two ends of a diene join into a ring and ethene is lost.","k":["Losing ethene gas drives the reaction.","Goes through a four-membered metallacyclobutane.","2005 Nobel Prize: Chauvin, Grubbs and Schrock."],"aka":"RCM olefin metathesis"},{"id":"g-yne-alkyl","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Alkylation of a terminal alkyne","s":"CC#C.CCBr","r":"1. NaNH₂  2. CH₃CH₂Br","p":"CC#CCC","sum":"A strong base removes the acidic alkyne hydrogen (pKa about 25), and the acetylide ion then attacks a halogenoalkane by SN2.","k":["Makes a new C–C bond and a longer alkyne.","Only methyl and primary halides work; secondary and tertiary ones give elimination."],"aka":"acetylide"},{"id":"g-yne-ald","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Acetylide addition to an aldehyde","s":"CC#C.O=CC","r":"1. NaNH₂  2. CH₃CHO  3. H₃O⁺","p":"CC#CC(C)O","sum":"The acetylide ion adds to a carbonyl carbon to give a propargylic alcohol.","k":["Works with aldehydes and ketones, like a Grignard reagent.","The acid work-up protonates the alkoxide."]},{"id":"g-lindlar","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Alkyne to cis alkene (Lindlar)","s":"CCC#CCC","r":"H₂, Lindlar catalyst","p":"CC/C=C\\CC","sum":"A poisoned palladium catalyst adds just one equivalent of hydrogen, to the same face, and then stops.","k":["Gives the cis (Z) alkene.","Lindlar catalyst is Pd on CaCO₃ poisoned with lead and quinoline."],"aka":"partial hydrogenation"},{"id":"g-dissolving","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Alkyne to trans alkene (dissolving metal)","s":"CCC#CCC","r":"Na, NH₃(l)","p":"CC/C=C/CC","sum":"Sodium in liquid ammonia adds electrons one at a time, and the intermediate vinyl radical settles into the trans shape.","k":["Gives the trans (E) alkene, the opposite of Lindlar.","Mechanism: electron, proton, electron, proton."],"aka":"Birch-type reduction"},{"id":"g-yne-h2","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Complete hydrogenation of an alkyne","s":"CCC#CCC","r":"excess H₂, Pd/C","p":"CCCCCC","sum":"With an ordinary catalyst the alkyne takes up two equivalents of hydrogen and becomes an alkane.","k":["The alkene is formed on the way but reacts faster than it can be isolated."]},{"id":"g-yne-h2o","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Hydration of an alkyne","s":"CCCC#C","r":"H₂SO₄, H₂O, HgSO₄","p":"CCCC(C)=O","sum":"Water adds Markovnikov to give an enol, which immediately tautomerises to a ketone.","k":["A terminal alkyne gives a methyl ketone.","Ethyne is the only alkyne that gives an aldehyde (ethanal)."],"m":"enol","aka":"Kucherov keto-enol tautomerism"},{"id":"g-yne-hb","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Hydroboration–oxidation of an alkyne","s":"CCCC#C","r":"1. Sia₂BH or 9-BBN  2. H₂O₂, NaOH","p":"CCCCC=O","sum":"Anti-Markovnikov hydration: a terminal alkyne becomes an aldehyde.","k":["A bulky borane adds only once and puts boron on the end carbon.","The enol formed tautomerises to the aldehyde."],"aka":"disiamylborane"},{"id":"g-yne-hx","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Addition of HX to an alkyne","s":"CCC#C","r":"excess HBr","p":"CCC(C)(Br)Br","sum":"HBr adds twice, and both bromines end up on the same carbon.","k":["One equivalent stops at the vinyl bromide.","Each addition follows Markovnikov’s rule, giving a geminal dihalide."]},{"id":"g-yne-x2","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Addition of halogen to an alkyne","s":"CC#CC","r":"Br₂ (1 equiv.)","p":"C/C(Br)=C(/C)Br","sum":"One equivalent of bromine adds anti to give the trans dibromoalkene.","k":["A second equivalent gives the tetrabromoalkane.","Alkynes react more slowly than alkenes with bromine."]},{"id":"g-yne-cleave","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Oxidative cleavage of an alkyne","s":"CCC#CC","r":"1. O₃  2. H₂O (or hot KMnO₄)","p":"CCC(=O)O.CC(=O)O","sum":"The triple bond is cut and each carbon becomes a carboxylic acid.","k":["A terminal ≡C–H carbon is lost as CO₂."]},{"id":"g-yne-make","cat":"Alkynes","name":"Alkyne from a dihalide","s":"CC(Br)C(C)Br","r":"excess NaNH₂, NH₃","p":"CC#CC","sum":"Two E2 eliminations in a row remove two HBr and leave a triple bond.","k":["Works from vicinal (1,2) or geminal (1,1) dihalides.","For a terminal alkyne use three equivalents of base, then add water."],"aka":"double dehydrohalogenation"},{"id":"g-pbr3","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Alcohol to bromoalkane with PBr₃","s":"CCCCO","r":"PBr₃","p":"CCCCBr","sum":"PBr₃ turns OH into a good leaving group, and bromide then displaces it by SN2.","k":["Inverts the stereocentre.","No carbocation, so no rearrangement. Best for primary and secondary alcohols.","Red phosphorus with Br₂ or I₂ makes PBr₃ or PI₃ in the flask."],"aka":"phosphorus tribromide"},{"id":"g-socl2","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Alcohol to chloroalkane with SOCl₂","s":"CCCCO","r":"SOCl₂, pyridine","p":"CCCCCl","sum":"Thionyl chloride converts an alcohol to a chloroalkane; the other products are gases.","k":["SO₂ and HCl bubble away, so the product is easy to purify.","With pyridine the stereocentre is inverted (SN2).","PCl₅ does the same job and gives steamy HCl fumes: a test for an OH group."],"aka":"thionyl chloride PCl5"},{"id":"g-roh-hx","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Tertiary alcohol with HCl","s":"CC(C)(C)O","r":"conc. HCl","p":"CC(C)(C)Cl","sum":"The OH is protonated, water leaves to give a carbocation, and chloride attacks.","k":["The Lucas test: tertiary alcohols go cloudy at once, secondary in minutes, primary not at all at room temperature.","Primary alcohols react with HBr by SN2 instead."],"m":"alcoholhx","aka":"Lucas reagent ZnCl2"},{"id":"g-appel","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Appel reaction","s":"CCCCO","r":"CBr₄, PPh₃","p":"CCCCBr","sum":"Triphenylphosphine and CBr₄ convert an alcohol to a bromoalkane under mild, neutral conditions.","k":["SN2, so the stereocentre is inverted.","Driven by forming the very strong P=O bond of Ph₃P=O."]},{"id":"g-tosyl","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Making a tosylate","s":"CCCCO","r":"TsCl, pyridine","p":"CCCCOS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1","sum":"Tosyl chloride turns the poor leaving group OH into tosylate, one of the best leaving groups.","k":["The C–O bond is not touched, so the stereocentre keeps its configuration.","The tosylate then reacts like a halogenoalkane in SN2 and E2 reactions.","Mesylates (MsCl) work the same way."],"aka":"sulfonate ester mesylate OTs"},{"id":"g-dehyd","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Dehydration of an alcohol","s":"OC1CCCCC1","r":"conc. H₂SO₄ or H₃PO₄, heat","p":"C1=CCCCC1","sum":"Acid protonates the OH, water leaves, and a neighbouring proton is lost to give an alkene.","k":["E1 for secondary and tertiary alcohols; the Zaitsev alkene is the major product.","Carbocation rearrangements are possible.","Passing the vapour over hot Al₂O₃ also works."],"m":"alcdehyd","aka":"elimination"},{"id":"g-pocl3","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Dehydration with POCl₃","s":"CC1(O)CCCCC1","r":"POCl₃, pyridine","p":"CC1=CCCCC1","sum":"POCl₃ turns OH into a good leaving group and pyridine removes a proton in an E2 step.","k":["No carbocation, so no rearrangement.","Works at 0 °C, much milder than hot acid."]},{"id":"g-pcc","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Oxidation of a primary alcohol to an aldehyde (PCC)","s":"CCCCO","r":"PCC, CH₂Cl₂","p":"CCCC=O","sum":"PCC oxidises a primary alcohol to an aldehyde and stops there, because no water is present.","k":["Secondary alcohols give ketones. Tertiary alcohols do not react.","Swern and Dess–Martin oxidations do the same job without chromium."],"aka":"pyridinium chlorochromate"},{"id":"g-swern","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Swern oxidation","s":"OCc1ccccc1","r":"1. (COCl)₂, DMSO, −78 °C  2. Et₃N","p":"O=Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Activated DMSO oxidises a primary alcohol to an aldehyde, or a secondary alcohol to a ketone.","k":["Metal-free and very mild, so it never over-oxidises to the acid.","By-products: dimethyl sulfide (which smells), CO and CO₂."]},{"id":"g-dmp","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Dess–Martin oxidation","s":"OC1CCCCC1","r":"Dess–Martin periodinane (DMP)","p":"O=C1CCCCC1","sum":"A hypervalent iodine reagent that oxidises alcohols at room temperature.","k":["Primary alcohol to aldehyde, secondary alcohol to ketone.","Neutral conditions, so acid-sensitive groups survive."]},{"id":"g-jones","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Oxidation of a primary alcohol to a carboxylic acid","s":"CCCCO","r":"K₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄, reflux (or CrO₃, H₂SO₄)","p":"CCCC(=O)O","sum":"In water the aldehyde formed first is hydrated and oxidised again, giving the carboxylic acid.","k":["Orange dichromate turns green (Cr³⁺).","To stop at the aldehyde, distil it out as it forms, or use PCC.","CrO₃ in aqueous H₂SO₄ and acetone is called the Jones reagent."],"aka":"Jones dichromate chromic acid"},{"id":"g-sec-ox","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Oxidation of a secondary alcohol to a ketone","s":"CCC(C)O","r":"K₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄, heat","p":"CCC(C)=O","sum":"A secondary alcohol loses two hydrogens to give a ketone, which cannot be oxidised further.","k":["Tertiary alcohols have no hydrogen on the carbinol carbon, so they are not oxidised.","The colour change from orange to green tells primary and secondary alcohols from tertiary ones."]},{"id":"g-alkoxide","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Making an alkoxide","s":"CCO","r":"Na (or NaH)","p":"CC[O-].[Na+]","sum":"Sodium metal or sodium hydride removes the OH proton to give an alkoxide and hydrogen gas.","k":["Alkoxides are strong bases and good nucleophiles.","The fizzing with sodium is a test for an OH group."],"aka":"ethoxide"},{"id":"g-tbs","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Protecting an alcohol as a silyl ether","s":"CCCCO","r":"TBSCl, imidazole","p":"CCCCO[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C","sum":"A bulky silyl group covers the OH so it survives bases, Grignard reagents, oxidants and hydrides.","k":["Removed with fluoride (TBAF): the Si–F bond is extremely strong.","TMS, TES and TIPS are related groups with different sizes."],"aka":"TBDMS protecting group TBAF deprotection"},{"id":"g-diol-cleave","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Cleavage of a 1,2-diol with periodate","s":"OC1CCCCC1O","r":"NaIO₄ (or HIO₄)","p":"O=CCCCCC=O","sum":"Periodate breaks the C–C bond between two neighbouring OH groups, leaving a C=O on each carbon.","k":["Goes through a cyclic periodate ester.","OsO₄ then NaIO₄ is an alternative to ozonolysis."],"aka":"periodic acid glycol cleavage Malaprade"},{"id":"g-mitsunobu","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Mitsunobu reaction","s":"CCC(C)O.OC(=O)c1ccccc1","r":"PPh₃, DEAD","p":"CCC(C)OC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"An alcohol is activated by PPh₃ and DEAD and displaced by an acidic nucleophile in one flask.","k":["Clean SN2: the alcohol carbon is inverted.","Hydrolysing the ester gives the alcohol with the opposite configuration."]},{"id":"g-disulfide","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Oxidation of thiols to a disulfide","s":"CCS.CCS","r":"I₂ (or air)","p":"CCSSCC","sum":"Mild oxidants join two thiols through an S–S bond.","k":["Disulfide bridges between cysteines hold proteins in shape.","Reduced back to thiols with zinc and acid, or with DTT."]},{"id":"g-sulfide","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Making a sulfide (thioether)","s":"CCS.CI","r":"1. NaOH  2. CH₃I","p":"CCSC","sum":"A thiolate ion is an excellent nucleophile and displaces a halide by SN2.","k":["Thiols (pKa about 10) are more acidic than alcohols, so NaOH is a strong enough base."]},{"id":"g-sulfoxide","cat":"Alcohols and thiols","name":"Oxidation of a sulfide","s":"CSC","r":"H₂O₂ (1 equiv.) or NaIO₄","p":"CS(C)=O","sum":"One equivalent of oxidant gives a sulfoxide.","k":["A second equivalent (or mCPBA in excess) gives the sulfone.","This sulfoxide is DMSO."]},{"id":"g-williamson","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Williamson ether synthesis","s":"CCC[O-].CI","r":"SN2","p":"CCCOC","sum":"An alkoxide attacks a methyl or primary halogenoalkane to make an ether.","k":["Choose the pair so the halide is the less hindered partner; a tertiary halide would just eliminate.","Make the alkoxide first with Na or NaH."],"m":"williamson"},{"id":"g-ether-dehyd","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Symmetrical ether from an alcohol","s":"CCO.CCO","r":"conc. H₂SO₄, 140 °C","p":"CCOCC","sum":"At a lower temperature than dehydration, one protonated alcohol is attacked by a second alcohol molecule.","k":["Only good for symmetrical ethers from primary alcohols.","At 170 °C the same mixture gives the alkene instead."]},{"id":"g-ether-cleave","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Cleavage of an ether with HBr or HI","s":"COc1ccccc1","r":"conc. HBr, heat","p":"Oc1ccccc1.CBr","sum":"Strong acid protonates the ether oxygen, and bromide attacks the less hindered carbon.","k":["An aryl ether always gives the phenol plus the alkyl halide, because SN2 cannot happen at a ring carbon.","With excess HX a dialkyl ether gives two alkyl halides.","BBr₃ does the same job under milder conditions."],"aka":"Zeisel demethylation"},{"id":"g-epox-base","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide opening with a strong nucleophile","s":"CC1CO1","r":"NaOCH₃, CH₃OH","p":"CC(O)COC","sum":"Under basic conditions the nucleophile attacks the less hindered carbon of the epoxide by SN2.","k":["Ring strain makes the epoxide reactive even though alkoxide is a poor leaving group.","Attack is from the back, so that carbon is inverted.","Works with HO⁻, RO⁻, CN⁻, RS⁻, amines, Grignard reagents and LiAlH₄."],"m":"epoxide"},{"id":"g-epox-acid","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide opening in acid","s":"CC1(C)CO1","r":"CH₃OH, H₂SO₄ (cat.)","p":"CC(C)(OC)CO","sum":"In acid the epoxide oxygen is protonated first, and the weak nucleophile attacks the more substituted carbon.","k":["The more substituted carbon carries more of the positive charge.","Still anti: the nucleophile comes in from the face opposite the oxygen.","Regiochemistry is the opposite of the base-mediated opening."],"m":"epoxideacid"},{"id":"g-epox-grig","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide opening with a Grignard reagent","s":"C1CO1.CC[Mg]Br","r":"1. dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCCO","sum":"A Grignard reagent opens the epoxide at the less hindered carbon, lengthening the chain by two carbons.","k":["Oxirane (ethylene oxide) gives a primary alcohol with two more carbons."]},{"id":"g-epox-lah","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide reduction with LiAlH₄","s":"CC1CO1","r":"1. LiAlH₄  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CC(C)O","sum":"Hydride attacks the less hindered carbon and opens the ring to an alcohol.","k":["The OH ends up on the more substituted carbon."]},{"id":"g-epox-nh3","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide opening with ammonia","s":"CC1CO1","r":"NH₃","p":"CC(O)CN","sum":"Ammonia or an amine attacks the less hindered carbon to give a 2-amino alcohol.","k":["Many beta-blocker drugs are made this way."]},{"id":"g-epox-make","cat":"Ethers and epoxides","name":"Epoxide from a halohydrin","s":"OCCCl","r":"NaOH","p":"C1CO1","sum":"Base removes the OH proton and the alkoxide displaces the halide on the next carbon: an intramolecular Williamson reaction.","k":["The O⁻ and the halogen must be anti to each other for the backside attack."]},{"id":"g-da","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Diels–Alder reaction","s":"C=CC=C.C=CC=O","r":"heat","p":"O=CC1CCC=CC1","sum":"A conjugated diene and an alkene (the dienophile) join in one step to make a cyclohexene.","k":["Two new C–C bonds and one new C=C form at once, through a ring of six moving electrons.","The diene must be able to reach the s-cis shape.","Fastest with an electron-poor dienophile (C=O, CN, NO₂) and an electron-rich diene."],"m":"dielsalder","aka":"[4+2] cycloaddition"},{"id":"g-da-anh","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Diels–Alder with maleic anhydride","s":"C=CC=C.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","r":"heat","p":"O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","sum":"The cis dienophile gives a cis-fused product: the stereochemistry of the alkene is kept.","k":["Stereospecific: cis substituents on the dienophile stay cis in the ring.","With a cyclic diene the endo product is favoured."],"aka":"endo rule"},{"id":"g-da-yne","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Diels–Alder with an alkyne","s":"C=CC=C.COC(=O)C#CC(=O)OC","r":"heat","p":"COC(=O)C1=C(C(=O)OC)CC=CC1","sum":"An alkyne dienophile gives a cyclohexa-1,4-diene.","k":["One of the alkyne’s two π bonds is used; the other stays as a C=C in the ring."]},{"id":"g-12add","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"1,2-Addition to a diene (kinetic)","s":"C=CC=C","r":"HBr, −80 °C","p":"CC(Br)C=C","sum":"Protonation gives an allylic cation. At low temperature bromide attacks the nearer carbon, giving the product that forms fastest.","k":["Kinetic control: the reaction is not reversible in the cold."]},{"id":"g-14add","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"1,4-Addition to a diene (thermodynamic)","s":"C=CC=C","r":"HBr, 40 °C","p":"C/C=C/CBr","sum":"When the mixture is warm the addition is reversible, and the more stable product, with the more substituted double bond, wins.","k":["Thermodynamic control.","Both products come from the same resonance-stabilised allylic cation."],"aka":"conjugate addition"},{"id":"g-cope","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Cope rearrangement","s":"C=CCC(C)C=C","r":"heat","p":"C=CCC/C=C/C","sum":"A 1,5-diene reshuffles its bonds through a six-membered ring transition state: a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift.","k":["One σ bond breaks, one forms and both π bonds move.","The equilibrium favours the more substituted alkene."],"aka":"sigmatropic"},{"id":"g-claisen-rearr","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Claisen rearrangement","s":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","r":"heat (about 200 °C)","p":"C=CCc1ccccc1O","sum":"An allyl aryl ether rearranges on heating: the allyl group moves from oxygen to the ortho carbon.","k":["A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift, followed by tautomerism back to the aromatic phenol.","Allyl vinyl ethers give γ,δ-unsaturated carbonyl compounds the same way."],"aka":"sigmatropic allyl phenyl ether"},{"id":"g-electro","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Electrocyclic ring closure","s":"C=C/C=C\\C=C","r":"heat","p":"C1=CC=CCC1","sum":"The ends of a conjugated triene join to make a ring, converting one π bond into a σ bond.","k":["With 6 π electrons, heat gives disrotatory closure and light gives conrotatory closure.","With 4 π electrons the rules are the other way round (Woodward–Hoffmann)."],"aka":"Woodward-Hoffmann disrotatory conrotatory"},{"id":"g-22","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"[2+2] Photocycloaddition","s":"C=C.C=C","r":"UV light","p":"C1CCC1","sum":"Two alkenes join into a cyclobutane when one is excited by UV light.","k":["Forbidden thermally but allowed photochemically.","This reaction joins neighbouring thymines in DNA after UV exposure."]},{"id":"g-click","cat":"Dienes and pericyclic","name":"Azide–alkyne cycloaddition (click reaction)","s":"CN=[N+]=[N-].C#Cc1ccccc1","r":"Cu(I) catalyst","p":"Cn1cc(-c2ccccc2)nn1","sum":"An azide and a terminal alkyne snap together into a 1,2,3-triazole ring.","k":["The copper catalyst gives only the 1,4-isomer, at room temperature, in water.","2022 Nobel Prize: Sharpless, Meldal and Bertozzi."],"aka":"CuAAC Huisgen 1,3-dipolar"},{"id":"g-nitration","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Nitration of benzene","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"conc. HNO₃, conc. H₂SO₄, 50 °C","p":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","sum":"Sulfuric acid turns nitric acid into the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺, which the ring attacks.","k":["Electrophilic aromatic substitution: attack, then loss of H⁺ to restore the aromatic ring.","Keep below 55 °C to avoid a second nitration."],"m":"nitration","aka":"EAS electrophilic substitution nitronium"},{"id":"g-ar-br","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Bromination of benzene","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"Br₂, FeBr₃","p":"Brc1ccccc1","sum":"A Lewis acid polarises Br₂ so that the stable benzene ring can attack it.","k":["FeBr₃ (or Fe filings, or AlBr₃) is the halogen carrier.","Chlorination uses Cl₂ with AlCl₃ or FeCl₃.","Substitution, not addition: the aromatic ring is kept."],"m":"brbenzene","aka":"halogenation halogen carrier"},{"id":"g-sulfonation","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Sulfonation of benzene","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"fuming H₂SO₄ (SO₃)","p":"OS(=O)(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"SO₃ (or HSO₃⁺) is the electrophile and gives benzenesulfonic acid.","k":["Reversible: heating with dilute acid removes the SO₃H group again.","So SO₃H can be used as a temporary blocking group."]},{"id":"g-fc-alk","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation","s":"c1ccccc1.CC(C)Cl","r":"AlCl₃","p":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","sum":"AlCl₃ pulls chloride off the halogenoalkane to make a carbocation, which the ring attacks.","k":["Carbocations rearrange: 1-chloropropane gives mostly the isopropyl product.","The product is more reactive than benzene, so polyalkylation is a problem.","Fails on rings carrying strongly deactivating groups such as NO₂."],"m":"fcalkyl"},{"id":"g-fc-acyl","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Friedel–Crafts acylation","s":"c1ccccc1.CC(=O)Cl","r":"AlCl₃","p":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"An acyl chloride and AlCl₃ give an acylium ion, RC≡O⁺, which adds to the ring to make an aryl ketone.","k":["No rearrangement, and it stops after one substitution because the ketone deactivates the ring.","Reduce the C=O afterwards (Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner) to get a straight-chain alkylbenzene."],"m":"fcacyl"},{"id":"g-nitro-red","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Reduction of a nitro group","s":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","r":"1. Sn, conc. HCl, heat  2. NaOH","p":"Nc1ccccc1","sum":"Tin and hydrochloric acid reduce nitrobenzene to the phenylammonium ion; alkali then frees phenylamine.","k":["H₂ with Pd/C, or Fe with HCl, also work.","Nitration then reduction is the standard route to aromatic amines."],"aka":"aniline phenylamine"},{"id":"g-clemmensen","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Clemmensen reduction","s":"CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","r":"Zn(Hg), conc. HCl, heat","p":"CCCc1ccccc1","sum":"Zinc amalgam in hot acid reduces a ketone C=O all the way to CH₂.","k":["Use it when the molecule tolerates acid; use Wolff–Kishner when it tolerates base.","Friedel–Crafts acylation then Clemmensen gives an unrearranged alkylbenzene."]},{"id":"g-wk","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Wolff–Kishner reduction","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"N₂H₄, KOH, heat","p":"C1CCCCC1","sum":"Hydrazine forms a hydrazone, and hot base then drives off nitrogen gas, leaving CH₂.","k":["Strongly basic conditions.","Loss of N₂ makes it irreversible."],"aka":"hydrazine"},{"id":"g-benz-ox","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Side-chain oxidation of an alkylbenzene","s":"Cc1ccccc1","r":"1. KMnO₄, heat  2. H₃O⁺","p":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"Hot manganate(VII) oxidises any alkyl side chain down to a COOH group on the ring.","k":["Whatever the chain length, the product is benzoic acid.","Needs at least one benzylic hydrogen: tert-butylbenzene does not react."],"aka":"permanganate benzoic acid"},{"id":"g-diazo","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Diazotisation","s":"Nc1ccccc1","r":"NaNO₂, HCl, 0–5 °C","p":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","sum":"Nitrous acid, made in the flask, converts a primary aromatic amine into a diazonium salt.","k":["Keep below 10 °C: the diazonium ion decomposes if it warms up.","N₂ is a superb leaving group, so the diazonium group can be replaced by many others."],"aka":"diazonium nitrous acid"},{"id":"g-sandmeyer","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Sandmeyer reaction (halide)","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"CuBr","p":"Brc1ccccc1","sum":"A copper(I) salt replaces the diazonium group with Cl, Br or CN, releasing nitrogen gas.","k":["CuCl gives the chloride, CuBr the bromide.","Lets you place groups in positions that direct substitution cannot reach."]},{"id":"g-sandmeyer-cn","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Sandmeyer reaction (nitrile)","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"CuCN","p":"N#Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Copper(I) cyanide replaces the diazonium group with CN.","k":["Hydrolysing the nitrile gives a benzoic acid."]},{"id":"g-diazo-oh","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Diazonium salt to phenol","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"H₂O, warm","p":"Oc1ccccc1","sum":"Warming the diazonium salt in water replaces N₂ with OH.","k":["This is why diazonium salts must be kept cold if you want to use them for coupling."]},{"id":"g-diazo-i","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Diazonium salt to aryl iodide","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"KI","p":"Ic1ccccc1","sum":"Iodide replaces the diazonium group without any copper catalyst.","k":["The easiest way to make an aryl iodide."]},{"id":"g-schiemann","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Balz–Schiemann reaction","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"1. HBF₄  2. heat","p":"Fc1ccccc1","sum":"The diazonium tetrafluoroborate salt is isolated and heated to give an aryl fluoride.","k":["One of the few practical routes to fluorobenzenes."]},{"id":"g-deamin","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Removing an amino group (H₃PO₂)","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1","r":"H₃PO₂","p":"c1ccccc1","sum":"Hypophosphorous acid replaces the diazonium group with hydrogen.","k":["Lets you use NH₂ as a directing group and then delete it."]},{"id":"g-azo","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Azo coupling","s":"N#[N+]c1ccccc1.Oc1ccccc1","r":"NaOH(aq), below 10 °C","p":"Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","sum":"The diazonium ion is a weak electrophile and attacks the activated ring of phenol at the 4-position.","k":["Gives a brightly coloured azo compound: the basis of azo dyes.","The colour comes from the long conjugated system through the N=N bond."],"m":"diazocoupling","aka":"azo dye"},{"id":"g-snar","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution (SNAr)","s":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Cl)cc1","r":"NaOCH₃, heat","p":"COc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","sum":"A nucleophile adds to the ring carbon bearing the halogen, then the halide leaves: addition–elimination.","k":["Needs an electron-withdrawing group ortho or para to the leaving group, to stabilise the anionic (Meisenheimer) intermediate.","Fluoride is the best leaving group here, the reverse of SN2."],"aka":"Meisenheimer addition-elimination"},{"id":"g-benzyne","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Substitution through benzyne","s":"Clc1ccccc1","r":"NaNH₂, NH₃(l)","p":"Nc1ccccc1","sum":"A very strong base removes HCl to give benzyne, which the nucleophile then adds to: elimination–addition.","k":["The new group can end up on the carbon next to where the halogen was.","Chlorobenzene with NaOH at 350 °C and high pressure gives phenol the same way (Dow process)."],"aka":"aryne elimination-addition Dow"},{"id":"g-birch","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Birch reduction","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"Na, NH₃(l), EtOH","p":"C1=CCC=CC1","sum":"Sodium in liquid ammonia adds two electrons and two protons to the ring, giving a non-conjugated cyclohexa-1,4-diene.","k":["Electron-donating groups end up on a C=C carbon; electron-withdrawing groups end up on an sp³ carbon."]},{"id":"g-eas-op","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Substitution of an activated ring (ortho/para)","s":"Cc1ccccc1","r":"conc. HNO₃, conc. H₂SO₄","p":"Cc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","sum":"Electron-donating groups make the ring more reactive and direct the new group to the 2- and 4-positions.","k":["Ortho/para directors: alkyl, OH, OR, NH₂, NHCOR and the halogens.","Methylbenzene reacts about 25 times faster than benzene and gives both the 2- and 4-nitro products.","Halogens are the exception: ortho/para directing but deactivating."],"aka":"directing effects activating"},{"id":"g-eas-meta","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Substitution of a deactivated ring (meta)","s":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","r":"conc. HNO₃, conc. H₂SO₄","p":"COC(=O)c1cccc(c1)[N+](=O)[O-]","sum":"Electron-withdrawing groups slow the reaction and direct the new group to the 3-position.","k":["Meta directors: NO₂, CN, COOH, COOR, CHO, COR, SO₃H and NR₃⁺.","Attack at the 2- or 4-position would put a positive charge next to the withdrawing group."],"aka":"directing effects deactivating"},{"id":"g-phenol-br","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Bromination of phenol","s":"Oc1ccccc1","r":"Br₂(aq)","p":"Oc1c(Br)cc(Br)cc1Br","sum":"Phenol is so activated that bromine water substitutes it three times with no catalyst.","k":["Bromine water is decolourised and a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol forms: a test for phenol.","In a non-polar solvent at low temperature you can stop at one bromine."],"m":"phenolbr"},{"id":"g-kolbe","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction","s":"Oc1ccccc1","r":"1. NaOH  2. CO₂, 125 °C, pressure  3. H₃O⁺","p":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1O","sum":"Sodium phenoxide attacks carbon dioxide at the ortho position to give 2-hydroxybenzoic (salicylic) acid.","k":["The industrial route to aspirin starts here."],"aka":"salicylic acid"},{"id":"g-reimer","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction","s":"Oc1ccccc1","r":"1. CHCl₃, NaOH(aq)  2. H₃O⁺","p":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","sum":"Dichlorocarbene, made from chloroform and alkali, attacks phenoxide at the ortho position; hydrolysis gives the aldehyde.","k":["Product: 2-hydroxybenzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde)."]},{"id":"g-gattermann","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Gattermann–Koch formylation","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"CO, HCl, AlCl₃, CuCl","p":"O=Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Carbon monoxide and HCl behave like methanoyl chloride and put a CHO group on the ring.","k":["An acylation that gives benzaldehyde."]},{"id":"g-ar-h2","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Hydrogenation of benzene","s":"c1ccccc1","r":"H₂, Ni, 150 °C, high pressure","p":"C1CCCCC1","sum":"Under forcing conditions benzene adds three molecules of hydrogen to give cyclohexane.","k":["Much harder than hydrogenating an alkene, because the aromatic stabilisation is lost.","The enthalpy change (−208 kJ mol⁻¹) is less than three times that of cyclohexene: evidence for delocalisation."]},{"id":"g-phenol-ester","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Ester from phenol","s":"Oc1ccccc1.CC(=O)Cl","r":"NaOH(aq) or pyridine","p":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","sum":"Phenol is too weak a nucleophile to be esterified by a carboxylic acid, so an acyl chloride or anhydride is used.","k":["Converting phenol to phenoxide first makes it a better nucleophile."]},{"id":"g-quinone","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Oxidation to a quinone","s":"Oc1ccc(O)cc1","r":"Na₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄","p":"O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1","sum":"Benzene-1,4-diol is easily oxidised to benzoquinone.","k":["Reversible: quinones are reduced back by mild reducing agents.","Ubiquinone (coenzyme Q) carries electrons in cells this way."]},{"id":"g-cumene","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Cumene process","s":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","r":"1. O₂  2. H₃O⁺","p":"Oc1ccccc1.CC(C)=O","sum":"Air oxidises cumene to a hydroperoxide, which rearranges in acid to phenol and propanone.","k":["The main industrial source of both phenol and propanone."],"aka":"phenol manufacture"},{"id":"g-fries","cat":"Aromatic","name":"Fries rearrangement","s":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","r":"AlCl₃, heat","p":"CC(=O)c1ccc(O)cc1","sum":"A phenyl ester rearranges with a Lewis acid: the acyl group moves from oxygen onto the ring.","k":["Low temperature favours the para product, high temperature the ortho product."]},{"id":"g-nabh4","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Reduction with NaBH₄","s":"CCC(C)=O","r":"NaBH₄, CH₃OH","p":"CCC(C)O","sum":"Borohydride delivers H⁻ to the carbonyl carbon; the solvent then protonates the oxygen.","k":["Aldehydes give primary alcohols, ketones give secondary alcohols.","Mild: does not reduce esters, acids, amides or C=C bonds."],"m":"nabh4","aka":"sodium borohydride tetrahydridoborate"},{"id":"g-lah","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Reduction with LiAlH₄","s":"CCCC=O","r":"1. LiAlH₄, dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCCO","sum":"A stronger hydride source than NaBH₄. It reduces aldehydes and ketones, and also acids, esters, amides and nitriles.","k":["Reacts violently with water, so use dry ether and add acid only afterwards.","Does not reduce isolated C=C bonds."],"m":"lialh4","aka":"lithium aluminium hydride tetrahydridoaluminate"},{"id":"g-grig-ket","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Grignard addition to a ketone","s":"CC(C)=O.CC[Mg]Br","r":"1. dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCC(C)(C)O","sum":"The carbon bonded to magnesium attacks the carbonyl carbon and makes a new C–C bond.","k":["Ketones give tertiary alcohols; aldehydes give secondary alcohols.","Acid is added in a separate second step."],"m":"grignard"},{"id":"g-grig-hcho","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Grignard addition to methanal","s":"C=O.CC[Mg]Br","r":"1. dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCO","sum":"Methanal is the only aldehyde that gives a primary alcohol, with one more carbon than the Grignard reagent.","k":["A simple way to add a CH₂OH group."],"aka":"formaldehyde"},{"id":"g-cyanohydrin","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Cyanohydrin formation","s":"CC(C)=O","r":"HCN, KCN","p":"CC(C)(O)C#N","sum":"Cyanide ion attacks the carbonyl carbon, and the alkoxide is then protonated.","k":["Nucleophilic addition, and it adds one carbon to the chain.","An aldehyde or unsymmetrical ketone gives a racemic mixture, because the flat C=O is attacked equally from both faces.","Hydrolysis of the nitrile gives a 2-hydroxy acid."],"m":"cyanohydrin","aka":"hydroxynitrile"},{"id":"g-hydrate","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Hydrate formation","s":"ClC(Cl)(Cl)C=O","r":"H₂O","p":"OC(O)C(Cl)(Cl)Cl","sum":"Water adds reversibly to a carbonyl group to give a geminal diol.","k":["For most ketones the equilibrium lies far to the left.","Electron-withdrawing groups favour the hydrate: methanal and trichloroethanal are almost fully hydrated."],"aka":"gem-diol chloral"},{"id":"g-hemiacetal","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Hemiacetal formation","s":"CC=O.CO","r":"CH₃OH, H⁺ (cat.)","p":"CC(O)OC","sum":"One molecule of alcohol adds across the C=O bond.","k":["Usually unstable, except five- and six-membered cyclic hemiacetals such as glucose."],"m":"hemiacetal"},{"id":"g-acetal","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Acetal formation","s":"O=C1CCCCC1.OCCO","r":"TsOH (cat.), remove water","p":"C1CCC2(CC1)OCCO2","sum":"Two alcohol OH groups replace the C=O oxygen. A diol gives a cyclic acetal.","k":["Acid-catalysed and reversible: remove water to push it forward.","Acetals are stable to bases, nucleophiles and hydrides, so they protect aldehydes and ketones."],"m":"acetal","aka":"ketal protecting group"},{"id":"g-acetal-hyd","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Acetal hydrolysis","s":"C1CCC2(CC1)OCCO2","r":"H₃O⁺","p":"O=C1CCCCC1.OCCO","sum":"Aqueous acid reverses acetal formation and gives the carbonyl compound back.","k":["This is the deprotection step."]},{"id":"g-imine","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Imine formation","s":"CC(C)=O.CN","r":"H⁺ (cat.), pH 4–5","p":"CC(C)=NC","sum":"A primary amine adds to the carbonyl group and water is then lost, leaving a C=N bond.","k":["Fastest around pH 4–5: enough acid to help water leave, but not so much that the amine is fully protonated.","Reversible: aqueous acid hydrolyses imines back."],"m":"imine","aka":"Schiff base"},{"id":"g-enamine","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Enamine formation","s":"O=C1CCCCC1.C1CCNC1","r":"H⁺ (cat.), remove water","p":"C1CCC(=CC1)N1CCCC1","sum":"A secondary amine has no second N–H to lose, so the proton is lost from the α-carbon instead, giving a C=C–N unit.","k":["Enamines are nucleophilic at the α-carbon and can be alkylated (Stork)."]},{"id":"g-oxime","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Oxime formation","s":"CC(C)=O","r":"NH₂OH","p":"CC(C)=NO","sum":"Hydroxylamine condenses with an aldehyde or ketone to give an oxime, C=N–OH.","k":["Same addition–elimination pattern as imine formation.","Oximes of cyclic ketones undergo the Beckmann rearrangement."],"aka":"hydroxylamine"},{"id":"g-dnph","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"2,4-DNPH test (hydrazone formation)","s":"CC(C)=O","r":"2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine","p":"CC(C)=NNc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","sum":"Aldehydes and ketones give an orange or yellow precipitate with 2,4-DNPH (Brady’s reagent).","k":["A condensation: addition, then loss of water.","The melting point of the purified solid identifies the original carbonyl compound.","Carboxylic acids and esters do not react."],"aka":"Brady reagent hydrazone"},{"id":"g-wittig","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Wittig reaction","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"Ph₃P=CH₂","p":"C=C1CCCCC1","sum":"A phosphorus ylide swaps the C=O oxygen for a carbon, putting the new C=C exactly where the carbonyl was.","k":["Goes through a four-membered oxaphosphetane, which collapses to the alkene and Ph₃P=O.","Unstabilised ylides give mainly Z alkenes; stabilised ylides give mainly E."],"m":"wittig","aka":"ylide olefination"},{"id":"g-hwe","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1","r":"(EtO)₂P(O)CH₂CO₂Et, NaH","p":"CCOC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","sum":"A phosphonate carbanion reacts with an aldehyde to give an α,β-unsaturated ester.","k":["Strongly E-selective.","The phosphate by-product dissolves in water, so it is easier to remove than Ph₃P=O."],"aka":"HWE olefination"},{"id":"g-redam","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Reductive amination","s":"O=C1CCCCC1.CN","r":"NaBH₃CN, pH 5","p":"CNC1CCCCC1","sum":"The carbonyl compound and amine form an iminium ion, which is reduced as it forms to give an amine.","k":["NaBH₃CN and NaBH(OAc)₃ reduce the iminium ion but leave the C=O alone.","The cleanest way to make secondary and tertiary amines: no over-alkylation."],"aka":"sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"id":"g-bv","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"mCPBA","p":"O=C1CCCCCO1","sum":"A peroxyacid inserts an oxygen atom next to the carbonyl: ketones become esters, cyclic ketones become lactones.","k":["The group that migrates to oxygen: tertiary > secondary ≈ phenyl > primary > methyl.","The migrating group keeps its configuration."],"aka":"lactone migratory aptitude"},{"id":"g-tollens","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Tollens’ test","s":"CCC=O","r":"[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺, warm","p":"CCC(=O)O","sum":"Aldehydes reduce silver(I) to silver metal, which coats the tube as a mirror. Ketones do not react.","k":["The aldehyde is oxidised to a carboxylate ion.","Tells aldehydes from ketones."],"aka":"silver mirror"},{"id":"g-fehling","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Fehling’s and Benedict’s tests","s":"CCC=O","r":"Cu²⁺ complex, OH⁻, warm","p":"CCC(=O)O","sum":"Aldehydes reduce the blue copper(II) complex to a brick-red precipitate of copper(I) oxide.","k":["Ketones give no change.","Reducing sugars such as glucose also give a positive test."],"aka":"copper(I) oxide reducing sugar"},{"id":"g-iodoform","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Iodoform (triiodomethane) test","s":"CCC(C)=O","r":"I₂, NaOH(aq)","p":"CCC(=O)O.IC(I)I","sum":"A methyl ketone is iodinated three times on the methyl group, and hydroxide then cleaves off CI₃⁻.","k":["Pale yellow precipitate of CHI₃ with an antiseptic smell.","Positive for CH₃CO– compounds and for CH₃CH(OH)– alcohols, which are oxidised to methyl ketones first.","Ethanal and ethanol are the only aldehyde and primary alcohol that respond."],"aka":"haloform"},{"id":"g-ald-ox","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Oxidation of an aldehyde","s":"CCCC=O","r":"K₂Cr₂O₇, H₂SO₄, reflux","p":"CCCC(=O)O","sum":"Aldehydes are easily oxidised to carboxylic acids; ketones resist oxidation.","k":["Orange dichromate turns green.","NaClO₂ (Pinnick oxidation) is a mild modern alternative."]},{"id":"g-cannizzaro","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Cannizzaro reaction","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1.O=Cc1ccccc1","r":"1. conc. NaOH  2. H₃O⁺","p":"OCc1ccccc1.OC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"An aldehyde with no α-hydrogen disproportionates in strong alkali: one molecule is oxidised and one is reduced.","k":["A hydride ion is passed directly from one aldehyde molecule to another.","Aldehydes with α-hydrogens give the aldol reaction instead."],"m":"cannizzaro","aka":"disproportionation"},{"id":"g-benzoin","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Benzoin condensation","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1.O=Cc1ccccc1","r":"NaCN (cat.), EtOH, H₂O","p":"OC(C(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","sum":"Cyanide turns one aldehyde carbon from an electrophile into a nucleophile, which then attacks a second aldehyde.","k":["A classic example of umpolung (reversed polarity).","Thiamine (vitamin B₁) catalyses the same reaction in cells."],"aka":"umpolung"},{"id":"g-thioacetal","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Thioacetal reduction (Mozingo)","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"1. HSCH₂CH₂SH, BF₃  2. Raney Ni","p":"C1CCCCC1","sum":"The ketone is converted to a dithioacetal, and Raney nickel then replaces both C–S bonds with C–H.","k":["Neutral conditions: a third way to turn C=O into CH₂."],"aka":"desulfurisation dithiane"},{"id":"g-bisulfite","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Addition of sodium hydrogensulfite","s":"CC=O","r":"NaHSO₃","p":"CC(O)S(=O)(=O)[O-].[Na+]","sum":"Hydrogensulfite adds to aldehydes and unhindered methyl ketones to give a crystalline salt.","k":["Used to separate and purify aldehydes: acid or base regenerates the aldehyde."],"aka":"bisulphite"},{"id":"g-mpv","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"Al(OiPr)₃, propan-2-ol","p":"OC1CCCCC1","sum":"Aluminium isopropoxide passes a hydride from propan-2-ol to the ketone through a six-membered ring.","k":["Very selective: C=C bonds and nitro groups are untouched.","The reverse reaction is the Oppenauer oxidation."],"aka":"Oppenauer"},{"id":"g-rosenmund","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Rosenmund reduction","s":"O=C(Cl)c1ccccc1","r":"H₂, Pd/BaSO₄","p":"O=Cc1ccccc1","sum":"A poisoned palladium catalyst reduces an acyl chloride to an aldehyde and no further.","k":["Barium sulfate (and a little sulfur or quinoline) stops the catalyst reducing the aldehyde."]},{"id":"g-stephen","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Stephen reduction","s":"CC#N","r":"1. SnCl₂, HCl  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CC=O","sum":"Tin(II) chloride and HCl reduce a nitrile to an iminium salt, which water hydrolyses to an aldehyde.","k":["DIBAL-H at −78 °C does the same conversion."]},{"id":"g-etard","cat":"Aldehydes and ketones","name":"Étard reaction","s":"Cc1ccccc1","r":"1. CrO₂Cl₂, CS₂  2. H₃O⁺","p":"O=Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Chromyl chloride oxidises the methyl group of methylbenzene to an aldehyde and stops there.","k":["Stronger oxidants such as KMnO₄ go on to benzoic acid."],"aka":"Etard chromyl chloride"},{"id":"g-fischer","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Fischer esterification","s":"CC(=O)O.OCC","r":"conc. H₂SO₄ (cat.), heat","p":"CC(=O)OCC","sum":"A carboxylic acid and an alcohol reach equilibrium with an ester and water.","k":["Reversible: use excess alcohol or remove water to improve the yield.","The alcohol’s oxygen ends up in the ester; the OH lost comes from the acid.","Mechanism: protonate, add, proton transfer, eliminate water, deprotonate."],"m":"fischer","aka":"esterification"},{"id":"g-sapon","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Saponification (base hydrolysis of an ester)","s":"CC(=O)OCC","r":"NaOH(aq), heat","p":"CC(=O)[O-].[Na+].OCC","sum":"Hydroxide attacks the ester carbonyl, and the alkoxide leaves. The acid formed is deprotonated at once.","k":["Irreversible, because the carboxylate ion will not react with the alcohol.","Add acid afterwards to get the free carboxylic acid.","Hydrolysing fats this way makes soap."],"m":"saponification","aka":"alkaline hydrolysis soap"},{"id":"g-ester-acid","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Acid hydrolysis of an ester","s":"CC(=O)OCC","r":"dilute H₂SO₄, heat","p":"CC(=O)O.OCC","sum":"The exact reverse of Fischer esterification.","k":["Reversible, so it does not go to completion: use a large excess of water."]},{"id":"g-acyl-make","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Making an acyl chloride","s":"CCC(=O)O","r":"SOCl₂ (or PCl₅)","p":"CCC(=O)Cl","sum":"Thionyl chloride swaps the OH of a carboxylic acid for Cl.","k":["By-products SO₂ and HCl are gases.","Acyl chlorides are the most reactive acid derivatives, so every other derivative can be made from them."],"aka":"acid chloride thionyl chloride"},{"id":"g-acyl-h2o","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Hydrolysis of an acyl chloride","s":"CC(=O)Cl","r":"H₂O","p":"CC(=O)O","sum":"Water attacks the carbonyl carbon and chloride leaves: nucleophilic addition–elimination.","k":["Fast and vigorous at room temperature, with steamy HCl fumes.","Much faster than hydrolysis of a chloroalkane or chlorobenzene."],"m":"acylhydrolysis"},{"id":"g-acyl-roh","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Ester from an acyl chloride","s":"CC(=O)Cl.OCC","r":"pyridine","p":"CC(=O)OCC","sum":"An alcohol attacks the acyl chloride and HCl is lost.","k":["Goes to completion at room temperature, unlike Fischer esterification.","Pyridine mops up the HCl."]},{"id":"g-acyl-amine","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Amide from an acyl chloride","s":"CC(=O)Cl.NCC","r":"room temperature","p":"CC(=O)NCC","sum":"An amine attacks the acyl chloride to give an amide.","k":["Two equivalents of amine are needed: the second one takes up the HCl.","Ammonia gives a primary amide; a primary amine gives an N-substituted amide."],"m":"amide"},{"id":"g-aspirin","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Acylation with an acid anhydride (aspirin)","s":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1O.CC(=O)OC(C)=O","r":"H₃PO₄ (cat.), warm","p":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1C(=O)O.CC(=O)O","sum":"The phenol OH of salicylic acid attacks ethanoic anhydride, and ethanoate leaves.","k":["Anhydrides are safer and cheaper than acyl chlorides, and the by-product is a carboxylic acid, not HCl."],"aka":"ethanoic anhydride acetic anhydride"},{"id":"g-amide-hyd","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Hydrolysis of an amide","s":"CC(=O)NC","r":"HCl(aq), reflux","p":"CC(=O)O.CN","sum":"Amides are the least reactive acid derivatives and need prolonged heating with acid or alkali.","k":["In acid: carboxylic acid plus the ammonium salt of the amine.","In alkali: carboxylate salt plus the free amine (or ammonia).","This is how peptide bonds are broken."]},{"id":"g-nitrile-hyd","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Hydrolysis of a nitrile","s":"CCC#N","r":"dilute HCl, reflux","p":"CCC(=O)O","sum":"Heating with aqueous acid converts C≡N into COOH, by way of the amide.","k":["With NaOH(aq) the product is the carboxylate salt and ammonia gas.","Combined with cyanide substitution, this adds one carbon and ends in an acid."]},{"id":"g-nitrile-red","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Reduction of a nitrile","s":"CCC#N","r":"1. LiAlH₄  2. H₂O (or H₂, Ni)","p":"CCCN","sum":"Four hydrogens add across the triple bond to give a primary amine.","k":["The amine has one more carbon than the halogenoalkane the nitrile was made from."]},{"id":"g-nitrile-grig","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Ketone from a nitrile and a Grignard reagent","s":"CC#N.CC[Mg]Br","r":"1. dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCC(C)=O","sum":"The Grignard reagent adds once to give an imine salt, which acid hydrolyses to a ketone.","k":["It adds only once, because the intermediate carries a negative charge on nitrogen."]},{"id":"g-ester-lah","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Reduction of an ester with LiAlH₄","s":"CCC(=O)OC","r":"1. LiAlH₄  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCO.CO","sum":"Two hydrides are delivered: the first gives an aldehyde, which is reduced at once to the primary alcohol.","k":["The OR part leaves as a second alcohol.","NaBH₄ is too weak to reduce esters."]},{"id":"g-acid-lah","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Reduction of a carboxylic acid","s":"CCCC(=O)O","r":"1. LiAlH₄, dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCCO","sum":"LiAlH₄ reduces a carboxylic acid to a primary alcohol.","k":["NaBH₄ does not reduce acids.","Borane (BH₃·THF) reduces acids selectively, even in the presence of esters."]},{"id":"g-amide-lah","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Reduction of an amide to an amine","s":"CC(=O)N(C)C","r":"1. LiAlH₄  2. H₂O","p":"CCN(C)C","sum":"Unlike other acid derivatives, an amide loses its oxygen: C=O becomes CH₂ and the nitrogen stays.","k":["Gives primary, secondary or tertiary amines depending on the amide."]},{"id":"g-dibal","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Ester to aldehyde with DIBAL-H","s":"CCCC(=O)OC","r":"1. DIBAL-H, −78 °C  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCCC=O.CO","sum":"One equivalent of a bulky hydride at low temperature adds once; the intermediate survives until the work-up.","k":["Keep it at −78 °C, or the aldehyde is reduced on to the alcohol.","DIBAL-H also reduces nitriles to aldehydes."],"aka":"diisobutylaluminium hydride"},{"id":"g-ester-grig","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Grignard addition to an ester","s":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1.C[Mg]Br.C[Mg]Br","r":"1. dry ether  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1.CO","sum":"Two equivalents add: the first makes a ketone, which is more reactive than the ester and reacts again.","k":["Product: a tertiary alcohol with two identical groups from the Grignard reagent.","You cannot stop at the ketone."]},{"id":"g-grig-co2","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Carboxylic acid from a Grignard reagent and CO₂","s":"CC[Mg]Br.O=C=O","r":"1. CO₂(s)  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCC(=O)O","sum":"The Grignard reagent attacks carbon dioxide to give a carboxylate, which acid protonates.","k":["Adds one carbon. Works for aryl and tertiary halides, where the cyanide route fails."],"aka":"carboxylation dry ice"},{"id":"g-dcc","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Amide from an acid and an amine (DCC coupling)","s":"CC(=O)O.NCc1ccccc1","r":"DCC","p":"CC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","sum":"DCC turns the acid OH into a good leaving group, so the amine can attack instead of just forming a salt.","k":["The standard way to make peptide bonds in the lab.","By-product: dicyclohexylurea, which precipitates."],"aka":"peptide coupling carbodiimide"},{"id":"g-aminolysis","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Amide from an ester (aminolysis)","s":"CC(=O)OC.N","r":"NH₃, heat","p":"CC(=O)N.CO","sum":"Ammonia or an amine displaces the alkoxide of an ester.","k":["Slower than with an acyl chloride, but no acid by-product."]},{"id":"g-transest","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Transesterification","s":"CC(=O)OC.OCC","r":"H⁺ or RO⁻ (cat.)","p":"CC(=O)OCC.CO","sum":"One alcohol group of an ester is exchanged for another.","k":["An equilibrium: use the new alcohol as the solvent.","Biodiesel is made by transesterifying vegetable oils with methanol."],"aka":"biodiesel"},{"id":"g-amide-dehyd","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Dehydration of an amide to a nitrile","s":"CCC(N)=O","r":"P₄O₁₀ (or SOCl₂), heat","p":"CCC#N","sum":"A strong dehydrating agent removes water from a primary amide.","k":["Only primary amides (–CONH₂) can do this."]},{"id":"g-decarb","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Decarboxylation of a β-keto acid","s":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","r":"heat","p":"CC(C)=O.O=C=O","sum":"A carboxylic acid with a C=O two carbons away loses CO₂ on gentle heating.","k":["Goes through a six-membered ring transition state to an enol, which becomes the ketone.","Malonic acids (1,3-diacids) do the same. Ordinary acids do not."]},{"id":"g-hvz","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction","s":"CCC(=O)O","r":"1. Br₂, PBr₃ (cat.)  2. H₂O","p":"CC(Br)C(=O)O","sum":"Bromine is placed on the α-carbon of a carboxylic acid.","k":["PBr₃ makes the acyl bromide, which enolises easily; the enol attacks Br₂.","Ammonia then converts the product into an α-amino acid."],"aka":"HVZ alpha bromination"},{"id":"g-acid-salt","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Carboxylic acid with a base","s":"CC(=O)O","r":"NaOH or NaHCO₃","p":"CC(=O)[O-].[Na+]","sum":"Carboxylic acids are weak acids and form salts with alkalis, carbonates and reactive metals.","k":["Fizzing with NaHCO₃ or Na₂CO₃ (CO₂) is the test for a carboxylic acid; phenols do not react.","The carboxylate ion is stabilised by delocalisation over both oxygens."],"m":"acidbase","aka":"neutralisation carbonate test"},{"id":"g-acyl-gilman","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Ketone from an acyl chloride and a Gilman reagent","s":"CCC(=O)Cl","r":"(CH₃)₂CuLi","p":"CCC(C)=O","sum":"A cuprate replaces Cl with an alkyl group and stops at the ketone.","k":["A Grignard reagent would add twice and give a tertiary alcohol."]},{"id":"g-ch2n2","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Methyl ester with diazomethane","s":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","r":"CH₂N₂","p":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"Diazomethane takes the acid proton, and the carboxylate then displaces N₂ from the methyldiazonium ion.","k":["Fast and clean, but diazomethane is toxic and explosive."]},{"id":"g-kolbe-el","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Kolbe electrolysis","s":"CC(=O)[O-].CC(=O)[O-]","r":"electrolysis","p":"CC","sum":"At the anode each carboxylate loses an electron and then CO₂; the two alkyl radicals join.","k":["Gives a symmetrical alkane with an even number of carbons."]},{"id":"g-sodalime","cat":"Acids and derivatives","name":"Decarboxylation with soda lime","s":"CC(=O)[O-].[Na+]","r":"NaOH, CaO, heat","p":"C","sum":"Heating a sodium carboxylate with soda lime removes the COO group, leaving an alkane with one carbon fewer.","k":["Sodium ethanoate gives methane; sodium benzoate gives benzene."]},{"id":"g-enol","cat":"Enolates","name":"Keto–enol tautomerism","s":"CC(C)=O","r":"H⁺ or OH⁻ (cat.)","p":"CC(O)=C","sum":"A carbonyl compound with an α-hydrogen is in equilibrium with a small amount of its enol.","k":["The keto form is normally much more stable.","The enol (or the enolate ion in base) is the nucleophile in every α-carbon reaction."],"m":"enol"},{"id":"g-aldol","cat":"Enolates","name":"Aldol addition","s":"CC=O.CC=O","r":"dilute NaOH, 5 °C","p":"CC(O)CC=O","sum":"The enolate of one aldehyde molecule attacks the carbonyl carbon of another.","k":["Product: a β-hydroxy aldehyde (an aldol).","Needs at least one α-hydrogen."],"m":"aldol"},{"id":"g-aldolcond","cat":"Enolates","name":"Aldol condensation","s":"CC(O)CC=O","r":"NaOH, heat","p":"C/C=C/C=O","sum":"On heating, the aldol loses water to give a conjugated α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compound.","k":["An E1cB elimination: the enolate forms first, then hydroxide leaves.","Conjugation of the C=C with the C=O drives it."],"m":"aldolcond","aka":"enone E1cB"},{"id":"g-claisen-schmidt","cat":"Enolates","name":"Crossed aldol (Claisen–Schmidt)","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1.CC(C)=O","r":"NaOH, EtOH","p":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","sum":"A ketone enolate attacks an aldehyde that has no α-hydrogens, so only one cross product forms.","k":["Benzaldehyde cannot form an enolate, and it is the better electrophile.","Dehydration follows at once because the product is conjugated with the ring."]},{"id":"g-claisen","cat":"Enolates","name":"Claisen condensation","s":"CC(=O)OCC.CC(=O)OCC","r":"1. NaOEt, EtOH  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O.CCO","sum":"An ester enolate attacks a second ester molecule and ethoxide leaves, giving a β-keto ester.","k":["Use the alkoxide that matches the ester, to avoid transesterification.","Needs a full equivalent of base: deprotonating the product pulls the equilibrium over."],"m":"claisen"},{"id":"g-dieckmann","cat":"Enolates","name":"Dieckmann condensation","s":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","r":"1. NaOEt  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O.CCO","sum":"An intramolecular Claisen condensation: a diester closes into a cyclic β-keto ester.","k":["Works best for five- and six-membered rings."]},{"id":"g-michael","cat":"Enolates","name":"Michael addition","s":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC.C=CC(C)=O","r":"NaOEt (cat.)","p":"CCOC(=O)C(CCC(C)=O)C(=O)OCC","sum":"A stabilised enolate adds to the β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compound (1,4-addition).","k":["Soft, stabilised nucleophiles add 1,4; hard ones such as Grignard reagents add 1,2 to the C=O.","Product: a 1,5-dicarbonyl compound."],"m":"michael","aka":"conjugate addition"},{"id":"g-robinson","cat":"Enolates","name":"Robinson annulation","s":"O=C1CCCCC1.C=CC(C)=O","r":"NaOEt, EtOH, heat","p":"O=C1C=C2CCCCC2CC1","sum":"A Michael addition followed by an intramolecular aldol condensation builds a new six-membered ring.","k":["Three C–C bonds change in one flask.","A classic route to the rings of steroids."]},{"id":"g-lda","cat":"Enolates","name":"α-Alkylation with LDA","s":"O=C1CCCCC1","r":"1. LDA, THF, −78 °C  2. CH₃I","p":"CC1CCCCC1=O","sum":"LDA removes an α-hydrogen completely, and the enolate then attacks a halogenoalkane by SN2.","k":["LDA is strong but too bulky to attack the C=O.","Use methyl or primary halides only."],"aka":"lithium diisopropylamide enolate alkylation"},{"id":"g-kinetic","cat":"Enolates","name":"Kinetic enolate alkylation","s":"CC1CCCCC1=O","r":"1. LDA, −78 °C  2. CH₃I","p":"CC1CCCC(C)C1=O","sum":"At low temperature the bulky base removes the less hindered α-hydrogen, so the new group goes on the less substituted side.","k":["Kinetic enolate: LDA, −78 °C. Thermodynamic (more substituted) enolate: weaker base, room temperature."],"aka":"thermodynamic enolate"},{"id":"g-malonic","cat":"Enolates","name":"Malonic ester synthesis","s":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC.CCBr","r":"1. NaOEt  2. CH₃CH₂Br  3. H₃O⁺, heat","p":"CCCC(=O)O","sum":"Diethyl malonate is alkylated, hydrolysed and decarboxylated to give a carboxylic acid with two more carbons than the halide.","k":["The CH₂ between two C=O groups (pKa about 13) is easily deprotonated by ethoxide.","Heating the diacid loses one CO₂.","Alkylate twice to make a disubstituted acid."]},{"id":"g-acetoacetic","cat":"Enolates","name":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis","s":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O.CCBr","r":"1. NaOEt  2. CH₃CH₂Br  3. H₃O⁺, heat","p":"CCCC(C)=O","sum":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated, hydrolysed and decarboxylated to give a methyl ketone.","k":["The same idea as the malonic ester synthesis, ending in a ketone."],"aka":"ethyl 3-oxobutanoate"},{"id":"g-alpha-hal","cat":"Enolates","name":"α-Halogenation of a ketone (acid)","s":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","r":"Br₂, CH₃COOH","p":"BrCC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"In acid the enol attacks bromine, putting one halogen on the α-carbon.","k":["Stops after one halogen in acid.","In base it carries on to the trihalo compound and then cleaves: the haloform reaction.","Rate does not depend on the bromine concentration: enol formation is the slow step."]},{"id":"g-stork","cat":"Enolates","name":"Stork enamine alkylation","s":"C1CCC(=CC1)N1CCCC1.CI","r":"1. CH₃I  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CC1CCCCC1=O","sum":"An enamine is alkylated on its α-carbon, and aqueous acid then hydrolyses it back to the ketone.","k":["Milder than using an enolate, and it stops after one alkylation."]},{"id":"g-cuprate","cat":"Enolates","name":"Conjugate addition of a Gilman reagent","s":"O=C1C=CCCC1","r":"1. (CH₃)₂CuLi  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CC1CCCC(=O)C1","sum":"An organocuprate adds its alkyl group to the β-carbon of an enone.","k":["Grignard and organolithium reagents attack the C=O directly instead (1,2-addition)."],"aka":"1,4-addition organocuprate"},{"id":"g-mannich","cat":"Enolates","name":"Mannich reaction","s":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1.C=O.CNC","r":"HCl (cat.)","p":"CN(C)CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"Methanal and an amine form an iminium ion, which the enol of a ketone attacks.","k":["Product: a β-amino ketone (a Mannich base).","Three components come together in one flask."]},{"id":"g-knoevenagel","cat":"Enolates","name":"Knoevenagel condensation","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1.CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","r":"piperidine (cat.)","p":"CCOC(=O)C(=Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)OCC","sum":"A doubly activated CH₂ group condenses with an aldehyde using only a weak amine base.","k":["Like an aldol condensation, but the nucleophile is acidic enough not to need hydroxide."]},{"id":"g-perkin","cat":"Enolates","name":"Perkin reaction","s":"O=Cc1ccccc1.CC(=O)OC(C)=O","r":"CH₃COONa, 180 °C","p":"OC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1.CC(=O)O","sum":"The enolate of an acid anhydride condenses with an aromatic aldehyde to give a cinnamic acid.","k":["An aldol-type condensation in which the anhydride provides the α-carbon."],"aka":"cinnamic acid"},{"id":"g-reformatsky","cat":"Enolates","name":"Reformatsky reaction","s":"CC(C)=O.BrCC(=O)OCC","r":"1. Zn  2. H₃O⁺","p":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)(C)O","sum":"Zinc inserts into an α-bromo ester to make a zinc enolate, which adds to a ketone or aldehyde.","k":["Product: a β-hydroxy ester.","The zinc enolate is too mild to attack the ester group itself."]},{"id":"g-amine-alk","cat":"Amines","name":"Amine from a halogenoalkane and ammonia","s":"CCBr","r":"excess NH₃, ethanol, heat (sealed tube)","p":"CCN","sum":"Ammonia displaces the halide, and a second ammonia molecule removes the extra proton.","k":["The amine formed is a better nucleophile than ammonia, so it reacts again to give secondary and tertiary amines and a quaternary salt.","A large excess of ammonia favours the primary amine."],"m":"nh3sub"},{"id":"g-gabriel","cat":"Amines","name":"Gabriel synthesis","s":"O=C1[N-]C(=O)c2ccccc12.BrCc1ccccc1","r":"1. DMF  2. N₂H₄","p":"NCc1ccccc1","sum":"The phthalimide anion is alkylated once, and hydrazine then releases the primary amine.","k":["The nitrogen is flanked by two C=O groups, so it cannot be alkylated a second time.","Gives primary amines only, with no over-alkylation."],"aka":"phthalimide"},{"id":"g-azide-red","cat":"Amines","name":"Reduction of an azide","s":"CCCN=[N+]=[N-]","r":"LiAlH₄, or H₂ and Pd/C","p":"CCCN","sum":"An alkyl azide loses N₂ on reduction to give a primary amine.","k":["SN2 with azide then reduction is a clean two-step route from a halogenoalkane to a primary amine.","PPh₃ and water does the same reduction (Staudinger)."],"aka":"Staudinger"},{"id":"g-hofmann-elim","cat":"Amines","name":"Hofmann elimination","s":"CCC(C)N","r":"1. excess CH₃I  2. Ag₂O, H₂O  3. heat","p":"CCC=C","sum":"The amine is methylated to a quaternary ammonium salt; heating its hydroxide gives an alkene.","k":["Gives the less substituted alkene: the bulky NMe₃⁺ group steers the base to the most accessible hydrogen.","An E2 elimination with trimethylamine as the leaving group."],"aka":"exhaustive methylation"},{"id":"g-cope-elim","cat":"Amines","name":"Cope elimination","s":"CCC(C)N(C)C","r":"1. H₂O₂  2. heat","p":"CCC=C.CN(C)O","sum":"A tertiary amine is oxidised to an amine oxide, which eliminates on heating through a five-membered ring.","k":["Syn elimination: the O⁻ takes a hydrogen from the same face.","Gives mainly the less substituted alkene."],"aka":"amine oxide"},{"id":"g-hinsberg","cat":"Amines","name":"Sulfonamide formation (Hinsberg test)","s":"CCN.O=S(=O)(Cl)c1ccccc1","r":"NaOH(aq)","p":"CCNS(=O)(=O)c1ccccc1","sum":"Amines attack benzenesulfonyl chloride to give sulfonamides.","k":["Primary amine: the sulfonamide dissolves in alkali. Secondary amine: insoluble solid. Tertiary amine: no reaction.","Sulfa drugs are sulfonamides."],"aka":"sulphonamide"},{"id":"g-amine-hno2","cat":"Amines","name":"Primary aliphatic amine with nitrous acid","s":"CCN","r":"NaNO₂, HCl","p":"CCO","sum":"An alkyl diazonium ion forms but falls apart at once, releasing nitrogen gas.","k":["Bubbles of N₂ are seen; the main organic product is the alcohol.","Aromatic diazonium ions are stable enough to use below 10 °C."]},{"id":"g-carbylamine","cat":"Amines","name":"Carbylamine (isocyanide) test","s":"CCN","r":"CHCl₃, KOH in ethanol, heat","p":"CC[N+]#[C-]","sum":"A primary amine reacts with dichlorocarbene to give an isocyanide with a very unpleasant smell.","k":["Only primary amines respond."],"aka":"isonitrile Hofmann isocyanide"},{"id":"g-amine-salt","cat":"Amines","name":"Amine with an acid","s":"CCN","r":"HCl","p":"CC[NH3+].[Cl-]","sum":"The nitrogen lone pair accepts a proton, giving an ionic ammonium salt.","k":["Base strength in water: aliphatic amines > ammonia > phenylamine.","In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring, so it is less available.","Alkali regenerates the free amine."],"aka":"basicity"},{"id":"g-eschweiler","cat":"Amines","name":"Eschweiler–Clarke methylation","s":"NCc1ccccc1","r":"HCHO, HCOOH, heat","p":"CN(C)Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Methanal forms an iminium ion and methanoic acid reduces it, methylating the amine.","k":["Stops at the tertiary amine: no quaternary salt forms."]},{"id":"g-boc","cat":"Amines","name":"Boc protection of an amine","s":"NCc1ccccc1","r":"Boc₂O, base","p":"CC(C)(C)OC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","sum":"The amine is converted to a carbamate, which is no longer basic or nucleophilic.","k":["Removed with acid (TFA or HCl), giving CO₂ and 2-methylpropene.","Used throughout peptide synthesis."],"aka":"tert-butoxycarbonyl carbamate protecting group"},{"id":"g-grig-h2o","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Grignard reagent with water","s":"CCCC[Mg]Br","r":"H₂O","p":"CCCC","sum":"A Grignard reagent is a very strong base and takes a proton from water, alcohols, acids, amines and terminal alkynes.","k":["This is why Grignard reactions must be kept dry.","With D₂O it is a way to put deuterium at a chosen carbon."]},{"id":"g-suzuki","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Suzuki coupling","s":"Brc1ccccc1.OB(O)c1ccc(C)cc1","r":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, Na₂CO₃","p":"Cc1ccc(cc1)-c1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium joins an aryl or vinyl halide to an organoboron compound.","k":["Catalytic cycle: oxidative addition, transmetalation, reductive elimination.","Needs a base to activate the boron.","2010 Nobel Prize (Suzuki, Heck, Negishi)."],"aka":"Suzuki-Miyaura cross-coupling boronic acid"},{"id":"g-heck","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Heck reaction","s":"Ic1ccccc1.C=CC(=O)OC","r":"Pd(OAc)₂, PPh₃, Et₃N","p":"COC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium couples an aryl or vinyl halide with an alkene, replacing a vinylic hydrogen.","k":["Gives the trans (E) alkene.","Steps: oxidative addition, alkene insertion, β-hydride elimination."],"aka":"Mizoroki-Heck"},{"id":"g-sonogashira","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Sonogashira coupling","s":"Ic1ccccc1.C#Cc1ccccc1","r":"Pd(PPh₃)₂Cl₂, CuI, Et₃N","p":"C(#Cc1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium and copper together couple a terminal alkyne with an aryl or vinyl halide.","k":["Copper makes a copper acetylide, which hands the alkyne to palladium."]},{"id":"g-stille","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Stille coupling","s":"Brc1ccccc1.C=C[Sn](CCCC)(CCCC)CCCC","r":"Pd(PPh₃)₄","p":"C=Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium couples an organotin compound with an aryl or vinyl halide.","k":["Tolerates almost every functional group, but tin compounds are toxic."],"aka":"organostannane"},{"id":"g-negishi","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Negishi coupling","s":"Ic1ccccc1.CC[Zn]Cl","r":"Pd(PPh₃)₄","p":"CCc1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium (or nickel) couples an organozinc reagent with an organic halide.","k":["Organozinc reagents allow sp³ carbons to be coupled."]},{"id":"g-kumada","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Kumada coupling","s":"Brc1ccccc1.C[Mg]Br","r":"Ni(dppp)Cl₂","p":"Cc1ccccc1","sum":"Nickel or palladium couples a Grignard reagent with an aryl or vinyl halide.","k":["The first cross-coupling of this kind, but limited by the reactivity of the Grignard reagent."]},{"id":"g-buchwald","cat":"Coupling and organometallics","name":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination","s":"Brc1ccccc1.C1COCCN1","r":"Pd₂(dba)₃, BINAP, NaOtBu","p":"C1CN(CCO1)c1ccccc1","sum":"Palladium joins an amine to an aryl halide, making an aryl C–N bond.","k":["Makes aryl amines that are hard to reach by SNAr or nitration and reduction."]},{"id":"g-pinacol","cat":"Rearrangements","name":"Pinacol rearrangement","s":"CC(C)(O)C(C)(C)O","r":"H₂SO₄","p":"CC(=O)C(C)(C)C","sum":"A 1,2-diol loses water to give a carbocation, and a neighbouring group shifts across to give a ketone.","k":["The shift is driven by forming a cation stabilised by the oxygen lone pair."],"aka":"pinacolone 1,2-shift"},{"id":"g-beckmann","cat":"Rearrangements","name":"Beckmann rearrangement","s":"ON=C1CCCCC1","r":"H₂SO₄, heat","p":"O=C1CCCCCN1","sum":"An oxime rearranges in acid to an amide: the group anti to the OH migrates to nitrogen as water leaves.","k":["Cyclohexanone oxime gives caprolactam, the monomer for nylon-6."],"aka":"caprolactam nylon"},{"id":"g-hofmann-rearr","cat":"Rearrangements","name":"Hofmann rearrangement","s":"CCC(N)=O","r":"Br₂, NaOH(aq)","p":"CCN","sum":"A primary amide loses its C=O carbon and becomes an amine with one carbon fewer.","k":["The alkyl group migrates from carbon to nitrogen to give an isocyanate, which water turns into the amine and CO₂.","The migrating group keeps its configuration."],"aka":"Hofmann bromamide degradation"},{"id":"g-curtius","cat":"Rearrangements","name":"Curtius rearrangement","s":"CCC(=O)N=[N+]=[N-]","r":"heat","p":"CCN=C=O","sum":"An acyl azide loses nitrogen gas on heating and rearranges to an isocyanate.","k":["Water converts the isocyanate to a primary amine and CO₂; an alcohol gives a carbamate."],"aka":"isocyanate"},{"id":"g-benzilic","cat":"Rearrangements","name":"Benzilic acid rearrangement","s":"O=C(C(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","r":"1. KOH  2. H₃O⁺","p":"OC(=O)C(O)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","sum":"Hydroxide adds to one C=O of a 1,2-diketone, and a phenyl group shifts to the neighbouring carbon.","k":["Product: an α-hydroxy acid."]},{"id":"g-sug-ring","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Ring closure of glucose","s":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"water","p":"OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H]1O","sum":"The OH on carbon 5 adds to the aldehyde group, giving a six-membered cyclic hemiacetal (a pyranose).","k":["In water over 99% of glucose is in the ring form.","The new stereocentre (the anomeric carbon) can be α or β; the two interconvert through the open chain (mutarotation).","β-Glucose has every substituent equatorial, so it is the more stable anomer."],"m":"hemiacetal","aka":"pyranose anomer mutarotation hemiacetal"},{"id":"g-sug-ox","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Oxidation of glucose to an aldonic acid","s":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"Br₂, H₂O","p":"O=C(O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","sum":"Bromine water oxidises only the aldehyde group, giving gluconic acid.","k":["Sugars that do this are reducing sugars: they also give positive Tollens’, Fehling’s and Benedict’s tests.","Sucrose has no free anomeric OH, so it is not a reducing sugar."],"aka":"gluconic acid reducing sugar"},{"id":"g-sug-hno3","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Oxidation of glucose to an aldaric acid","s":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"dilute HNO₃, heat","p":"O=C(O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","sum":"Nitric acid oxidises both ends of the chain, the CHO and the CH₂OH, to COOH.","k":["Product: glucaric acid.","Some aldaric acids are meso, which Fischer used to work out the structures of the sugars."],"aka":"glucaric saccharic acid"},{"id":"g-sug-red","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Reduction of glucose to an alditol","s":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"NaBH₄, H₂O","p":"OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)CO","sum":"Borohydride reduces the aldehyde of the open-chain form to CH₂OH, giving glucitol (sorbitol).","k":["The small amount of open-chain form reacts and the ring keeps reopening to replace it.","Sorbitol is used as a sweetener."],"aka":"sorbitol glucitol"},{"id":"g-sug-glyc","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Glycoside formation","s":"OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H]1O","r":"CH₃OH, HCl","p":"CO[C@@H]1O[C@H](CO)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H]1O","sum":"Only the anomeric OH is replaced: the hemiacetal becomes an acetal, called a glycoside.","k":["Both α and β glycosides form.","Glycosides do not mutarotate and are not reducing sugars.","The glycosidic links in starch, cellulose and sucrose are acetals of this kind."],"m":"acetal","aka":"Fischer glycosidation acetal"},{"id":"g-sug-ac","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Acetylation of glucose","s":"OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H]1O","r":"excess (CH₃CO)₂O, pyridine","p":"CC(=O)OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](OC(C)=O)[C@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H]1OC(C)=O","sum":"Every OH group is converted to an ester, giving glucose pentaacetate.","k":["Forming a pentaacetate was early evidence that glucose has five OH groups."],"aka":"pentaacetate"},{"id":"g-kiliani","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Kiliani–Fischer synthesis","s":"O=C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"1. HCN  2. H₂, Pd/BaSO₄  3. H₃O⁺","p":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","sum":"Lengthens an aldose by one carbon at the aldehyde end, by way of a cyanohydrin.","k":["The new stereocentre forms both ways, so arabinose gives glucose and mannose (C-2 epimers)."],"m":"cyanohydrin","aka":"chain extension epimer"},{"id":"g-wohl","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Wohl degradation","s":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","r":"1. NH₂OH  2. (CH₃CO)₂O  3. NaOCH₃","p":"O=C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO","sum":"Shortens an aldose by one carbon: the aldehyde becomes a nitrile through its oxime, and HCN is then lost.","k":["The reverse of the Kiliani–Fischer synthesis.","Glucose and mannose both give arabinose."],"aka":"chain shortening"},{"id":"g-strecker","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Strecker synthesis of an amino acid","s":"CC=O","r":"1. NH₄Cl, KCN  2. H₃O⁺, heat","p":"CC(N)C(=O)O","sum":"An aldehyde, ammonia and cyanide give an α-amino nitrile, which is hydrolysed to the amino acid.","k":["Imine formation, then cyanide addition, then nitrile hydrolysis.","The product is racemic."]},{"id":"g-zwitterion","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Zwitterion formation","s":"NCC(=O)O","r":"in water, at the isoelectric point","p":"[NH3+]CC(=O)[O-]","sum":"The COOH group gives its proton to the NH₂ group in the same molecule.","k":["Explains the high melting points and water solubility of amino acids.","In acid the ion is positive overall; in alkali it is negative."],"aka":"isoelectric point"},{"id":"g-peptide","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Peptide bond formation","s":"NCC(=O)O.C[C@H](N)C(=O)O","r":"condensation","p":"C[C@H](NC(=O)CN)C(=O)O","sum":"The COOH of one amino acid and the NH₂ of another join through an amide (peptide) bond, losing water.","k":["Two different amino acids can join in two orders, so the lab synthesis uses protecting groups and a coupling agent such as DCC.","Proteins are polyamides built this way."],"aka":"dipeptide condensation amide"},{"id":"g-pep-hyd","cat":"Sugars and amino acids","name":"Hydrolysis of a peptide","s":"C[C@H](NC(=O)CN)C(=O)O","r":"6 mol dm⁻³ HCl, reflux","p":"NCC(=O)O.C[C@H](N)C(=O)O","sum":"Prolonged heating with acid breaks every peptide bond and releases the amino acids.","k":["In acid the amino acids are obtained as their ammonium salts.","The mixture can then be separated by chromatography or electrophoresis."]}]};
